Juniper: She just wants tomorrow to be worth remembering. I can’t say I blame her optimistic spirit.
Noah: Oh, it’ll be a night to remember...
Noah: Now, if you’ll excuse me, I’ve got to go into the basement for no reason what-so-ever.
Lizzy: I guess we’ve gotta add finding dresses to our evening plans.
Juniper: My mother probably has something we can borrow.
Juniper: If not, we can make a statement by going in our regular clothes as a commentary on the unreasonable expectation for women to perform perfect femininity.
Lizzy: Took the words right out of my mouth.
Juniper: I’m going to go find Barb and makes sure she’s not too upset. Meet in the library in like an hour?
Lizzy: Sounds good. Don’t go easier on her! Let her stew a bit! She could use it.

Valerie: Ah, Elizabeth, how was your last day of highschool? I heard whisperings in the staff room that Holiday gave you a math exam.
Lizzy: It was pretty brutal.
Valerie: Hm. Well, math will only get you so far in your, shall I say, career. It was wise of you to focus on baseball. Speaking of, I circled the most useful classes in the fax I sent you. History, naturally, is my most recommended.
Lizzy: Val, is this really all there is?
Valerie: Oh, no, there are many different classes. I’m sure you could squeeze in an art class or maybe theatre to give yourself something fun to do. And VCR’s sports teams comes highly regarded. They even have baseball.
Lizzy: No, not in school, I mean in life. Am I doomed to make every waking moment about killing some old dudes?
Valerie: The vampire lords aren’t some old dudes. They’re also old dudettes.
Lizzy: You must’ve had dreams outside of prophecies and the keepers.
Valerie: This watch belonged to my great-great-grandmother, back when the Keep was first started. The eldest of every generation inherits that responsibility and the watch is a symbol of the struggle between light and darkness and of course, helps me get to appointments on time. Plus it’s been known to be quite lucky.
Lizzy: Yeah the watch is cool and all, but...
Lizzy: Didn’t you want to do literally anything else?
Valerie: Hm.
Valerie: ...
Valerie: No.
Lizzy: So you’re entirely happy doing exactly what you’re doing and you’re going to do it for the rest of your life?
Valerie: I am.
Lizzy: I don’t know why I bothered asking.
Valerie: There was a time in my life where I questioned what I was doing, if it was worth continuing my family’s duty.
Valerie: The Keep has it’s fair share of problems.
I believe it was my questioning that allowed me to break the rules and start working with you. Originally I was sent to Vermilion to observe and if necessary, eliminate threats to humanity, at my discretion.
Lizzy: Uh... Thanks for not... killing me?
Valerie: You’re doing so much good here, all of you, Juniper, Barb, and even Noah. Without you and your friends, the Vermilion Event Horizon would have swallowed up half the country by now.
Lizzy: But what do I tell my parents? Sorry mom, I can’t go to Emmerdale because I have to stop Dracula from killing the president?
Valerie: I see. You’re worried about losing touch with everyone.
Lizzy: Not exactly, but I guess that’s a little on my mind.
Valerie: They care for you deeply, Elizabeth. Juniper isn’t the type of person to abandon those she loves. Barb is fiercely loyal, and Noah is enrolling in VCR’s arts program, so you’ll have a familiar face. Besides, situations like these are exactly why portable telephones and electronic mail was made—even if I disagree with the discarding of paper post.
Lizzy: But Emmerdale is half way across the United States! What if they need me. I can’t kill a vampire through the computer. Not yet anyway. What if some crazed fishman attacks Barb or Juniper sees a vision about impending doom and I’m too busy writing an essay about an old book nobody reads anymore—
Valerie: Thing are comfortable right now. And I’m sure we could spend another two or three years operating exactly as we are now, hunting vampires, saving people...
Valerie: but there wouldn’t be anything to challenge us. We’d be stuck viewing the world through the lens of highschool student and teacher librarian, when it’s so much bigger than that.
Lizzy: Of course as soon as I’ve got the whole monster hunting thing figured out I have to go and refigure it all out again.
Valerie: You’re more capable than you think.


